## Approach

### Architecture Decision: Monorepo + Microservice Patterns

**Why monorepo?**
- Single source of truth for domain types (Order, User, Menu)
- Shared types between frontend and backend prevent API contract drift
- One `pnpm install`, one lockfile, one deployment pipeline
- Type changes cascade automatically across all consumers

```
greenfoood/ (monorepo)
├── apps/server/          # Express API (port 4000)
├── apps/web/             # Next.js frontend (port 3000)
└── packages/shared/      # TypeScript types (@greenfood/shared)
```

### Technology Stack

| Layer | Choice | Rationale |
|-------|--------|-----------|
| **Server** | Express + TypeScript | Lightweight, type-safe, battle-tested |
| **Database** | PostgreSQL + Prisma | ACID compliance, migrations, type safety |
| **Real-time** | Socket.IO + WebSockets | Instant updates, fallback for older browsers |
| **Frontend** | Next.js 14 (App Router) | Server components, route groups by role |
| **State** | Zustand | Minimal boilerplate for cart/orders |
| **Auth** | JWT + httpOnly cookies | Stateless, CSRF-protected, refresh rotation |

**Key principle:** Use proven, stable technologies; avoid hype. Optimize for maintainability and type safety.

### Core Architecture Patterns

#### 1. Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)

**Problem:** Multi-role app (customer, kitchen, driver, admin) needs different views and permissions.

**Solution:**
```
Frontend:
  ├── (client)        → Menu/Cart/Checkout (unauthenticated)
  ├── (kitchen)       → Live order display (KITCHEN role)
  ├── (driver)        → Delivery assignments (DRIVER role)
  └── (admin)         → Dashboard (ADMIN role)

Backend:
  Middleware: jwt.verify(token) → attach req.user.role
  Guards: requireRole("ADMIN", "KITCHEN") on protected routes
  Socket.IO: room subscriptions per role (kitchen, driver:123, order:456)
```

**Result:** Each role sees only what they need; no permission bleeding.

#### 2. QR-Based Ordering System

**Problem:** Dine-in customers must flag down staff to order; staff interruptions disrupt kitchen.

**Solution:**
```
Each table gets unique QR code (qrToken in database)
Customer scans → views menu → adds to cart → places order
Order appears in kitchen in real-time
Customer watches status on their phone
```

**Database:**
```prisma
Table { id, number, qrToken, restaurantId }
Order { id, tableId, type (DINE_IN|DELIVERY|TAKEAWAY) }
```

**Result:** Self-service ordering, zero staff interaction for order placement, reduced queue times.

#### 3. Real-Time Order Pipeline with Socket.IO

**Problem:** Kitchen doesn't know new orders; customers don't know order status; drives need assignment notifications.

**Solution:**
```
Customer places order (POST /api/orders)
    ↓
Server creates Order in DB
    ↓
Emits to "kitchen" room → Kitchen display updates instantly
    ↓
Customer joins "order:123" room
    ↓
Kitchen marks PREPARING → Server emits to order:123
    ↓
Customer's phone updates in real-time (no refresh needed)
```

**Event Rooms:**
- `kitchen` — all new orders
- `order:<id>` — specific order updates
- `driver:<id>` — delivery assignments for driver
- `admin` — system alerts

**Result:** <100ms latency for order updates; eliminates polling; kitchen never misses orders.

#### 4. Delivery Management Workflow

**Problem:** No way to assign, track, or prioritize deliveries.

**Solution:**
```
Admin views unassigned delivery orders
    ↓
Clicks "Assign Driver" → Driver selected
    ↓
Driver gets notification (Socket.IO to driver:123 room)
    ↓
Driver accepts → Order status changes to ACCEPTED
    ↓
Driver marks PICKED_UP → Customers notified
    ↓
Driver marks DELIVERED → Order complete
    ↓
Timestamps logged: assignedAt, acceptedAt, pickedUpAt, deliveredAt
```

**Database:**
```prisma
DeliveryAssignment {
  orderId, driverId, assignedAt, acceptedAt, pickedUpAt, deliveredAt
}
```

**Result:** Full delivery visibility, SLA tracking, driver accountability, customer satisfaction.

#### 5. Menu Management with Dynamic Options

**Problem:** Menu items have variable options (size, toppings, sides) that need flexible pricing.

**Solution:**
```
MenuItem { id, name, price, available, rating, calories, image }
  ↓ 1:n
MenuItemOption { label, price }
  // e.g., Size M (+$0), Size L (+$1.50)

OrderItem { quantity, unitPrice, selectedOpts: JSON }
  // Customer chose: Medium Burger with Cheddar & Bacon = $9.99
  // selectedOpts = [{ label: "Cheddar", price: 0.50 }, { label: "Bacon", price: 0.75 }]
```

**Admin Panel:**
- Create/edit categories
- Add/remove items, set availability
- Define options and surcharges
- Reorder by position for display priority

**Result:** Flexible menu without schema migrations; admins control everything without code changes.

### Implementation Highlights

#### Authentication Flow

```typescript
// 1. Login
POST /api/auth/login { email, password }
  → Validate passwordHash (bcrypt)
  → Generate JWT (access token, short-lived)
  → Set httpOnly cookie (refresh token, long-lived)
  → Return { token, user: { id, role } }

// 2. Protected routes
GET /api/orders
  Authorization: Bearer <access_token>
  Middleware: jwt.verify(token) → attach req.user
  Guard: requireRole("ADMIN", "KITCHEN")
  → Success or 403 Forbidden

// 3. Next.js middleware
Every request to /admin/*, /kitchen/*, etc.
  → Check gf_auth cookie for role
  → Redirect if role mismatch
  → Prevents accidental access to wrong dashboard
```

#### Order Creation with Real-Time Broadcast

```typescript
// apps/server/src/services/order.service.ts
async createOrder(items, type, tableId?, deliveryAddress?) {
  // 1. Validate: items exist, prices match
  const order = await db.order.create({
    data: {
      items: { create: items },
      type, tableId, deliveryAddress,
      total: calculateTotal(items)
    },
    include: { items: { include: { menuItem: true } } }
  });

  // 2. Notify kitchen instantly
  this.io.to("kitchen").emit("newOrder", {
    order,
    prepTime: estimatePrepTime(order.items)
  });

  // 3. Customer joins their order room
  // (happens on frontend after order succeeds)

  return order;
}
```

#### Kitchen Display System

```typescript
// apps/web/src/app/(kitchen)/kitchen/display/page.tsx
export default function KitchenDisplay() {
  const [orders, setOrders] = useState<Order[]>([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    const socket = io(SOCKET_URL);

    // Subscribe to new orders
    socket.on("newOrder", (order) => {
      setOrders((prev) => [order, ...prev]);
      playNotificationSound(); // Alert kitchen
    });

    // Subscribe to order updates (status changes)
    socket.on("orderUpdate", (order) => {
      setOrders((prev) =>
        prev.map((o) => (o.id === order.id ? order : o))
      );
    });

    return () => socket.disconnect();
  }, []);

  return (
    <div className="grid grid-cols-3 gap-4">
      {orders
        .filter((o) => o.status !== "DELIVERED")
        .sort((a, b) => {
          // Show PENDING first, then by age
          if (a.status === "PENDING") return -1;
          return a.createdAt - b.createdAt;
        })
        .map((order) => (
          <OrderCard key={order.id} order={order} />
        ))}
    </div>
  );
}
```

#### Real-Time Cart State (Zustand)

```typescript
// apps/web/src/store/cart.ts
export const useCartStore = create<CartState>((set, get) => ({
  items: [],

  addItem: (menuItem, quantity, selectedOpts) =>
    set((state) => ({
      items: [
        ...state.items,
        { menuItem, quantity, selectedOpts, id: nanoid() }
      ]
    })),

  removeItem: (itemId) =>
    set((state) => ({
      items: state.items.filter((i) => i.id !== itemId)
    })),

  getTotal: () => {
    const items = get().items;
    return items.reduce((sum, item) => {
      const basePrice = item.menuItem.price;
      const optionsPrice = item.selectedOpts.reduce(
        (s, o) => s + o.price,
        0
      );
      return sum + (basePrice + optionsPrice) * item.quantity;
    }, 0);
  }
}));
```

### Database Design

**Schema Highlights:**

```prisma
// Users & Auth
User { id, email, passwordHash, name, role (ADMIN|KITCHEN|DRIVER) }

// Restaurant Structure
Restaurant { id, name, tables[] }
Table { id, number, qrToken (unique), restaurantId, orders[] }

// Menu
Category { id, name, icon, position }
MenuItem { id, name, price, image, available, rating, categoryId, options[] }
MenuItemOption { id, label, price, menuItemId }

// Orders & Items
Order {
  id, status (PENDING→ACCEPTED→PREPARING→READY→PICKED_UP→DELIVERED)
  type (DINE_IN|TAKEAWAY|DELIVERY)
  tableId? (for dine-in), deliveryAddress? (for delivery)
  total, createdAt, updatedAt
}
OrderItem {
  id, quantity, unitPrice, selectedOpts (JSON), orderId, menuItemId
}

// Delivery
DeliveryAssignment {
  id, orderId (unique), driverId
  assignedAt, acceptedAt, pickedUpAt, deliveredAt
}
```

**Indexing Strategy:**
- `Order.status` — kitchen filters "all PENDING"
- `Order.createdAt` — timeline queries
- `Order.type` — order type filtering
- `MenuItem.available` — menu doesn't show unavailable items
- `MenuItem.categoryId` — fetch by category
- `Table.qrToken` — customer QR lookup
- `User.role` — find staff by role

### Development Workflow

```bash
# Install dependencies
pnpm install

# Start database
docker compose up -d

# Run both server and web concurrently
pnpm dev
# → Server runs on http://localhost:4000
# → Web runs on http://localhost:3000

# Database tools
pnpm db:migrate   # Apply Prisma migrations
pnpm db:seed      # Seed demo data
pnpm db:studio    # Visual database explorer
```

**Seed Data:**
```
Accounts:
  admin@greenfood.com / admin123     (ADMIN)
  kitchen@greenfood.com / kitchen123 (KITCHEN)
  driver@greenfood.com / driver123   (DRIVER)

Restaurant:
  "Main Street Bistro" with 8 tables
  Sample menu: Appetizers, Mains, Desserts, Drinks
```

